WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.680
Scientists at NVIDIA knocked it out&nbsp;
of the park with this one. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:16.880
they are training an AI that moves this virtual&nbsp;
robot arm to finish everyday tasks on this table.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:27.680
Let’s see. It picks up the toy school bus. So&nbsp;
far so good. But, wait a second! This is not good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.840
at all! It should have grabbed the c-clamp&nbsp;
here, not the toy school bus. Why is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:42.240
Well, it has only seen one demonstration to learn&nbsp;
from yet, so these are just the humble beginnings.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:52.640
Now, let’s look at what it can do after seeing 10&nbsp;
demonstrations. And…yes! That is so cool! Learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:59.840
is happening - The c-clamp goes in the box&nbsp;
successfully. Wonderful! And now, for the crayon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:11.840
box…that is not a crayon box. And neither is that&nbsp;
one. And it’s not that one either. Oh my goodness.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:20.640
Now, hold on to your papers, and let’s see what it&nbsp;
learned after looking at still as few as a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:32.000
demonstrations. C-clamp into the box, crayon&nbsp;
box too, toy school bus, get in that box. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:43.200
yes, finally! The rest seems to work&nbsp;
fine too. I love it. Good job, little AI!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.040
And if you were wondering,&nbsp;
this is a porcelain plate.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:54.160
And that is twist number one. Learning&nbsp;
happening from very few image-action pairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:04.160
How many exactly? Well, it learned to do&nbsp;
this, and more, from only 179 image-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:10.960
pairs. That, in the era of learning-based&nbsp;
techniques, is almost nothing at all. Wow.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.680
And, it gets better. One of&nbsp;
the most mind-blowing results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:23.680
is that from these very few examples, it also&nbsp;
learned to deal with previously unseen colors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:32.800
words. And not only that, but it was also able&nbsp;
to learn a wide variety of tasks from these 179&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:40.160
examples. And its capabilities just keep&nbsp;
going on and on and on. Absolutely amazing.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:47.600
Okay, now, that is all well and&nbsp;
good. But still, this is robot arm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:55.440
can play a computer game. Is that it? Why make&nbsp;
a video about this? Why is this so important?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:02.800
Well, and now, I give you the two more twists&nbsp;
that breathe new life into this project.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.520
Twist number two. We can use natural language&nbsp;
to give instructions to these robots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:19.360
Yes! And twist number three. If you have&nbsp;
been holding on to your papers so far, now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:26.640
squeeze that paper, because the knowledge it&nbsp;
learned in the simulation transfers to reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:33.760
We come come out of the video game world into the&nbsp;
real world, and the AI still works wonderfully.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:43.280
Let’s have a look. It can move chess pieces. Great&nbsp;
work little AI, I love it! But, this was a solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:50.480
chess piece, you know what, let’s try something&nbsp;
that requires a little more delicacy. Let’s see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:58.480
Yes, folding and unfolding a piece of&nbsp;
cloth is also not a problem. That requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.840
delicate movement. So good, I love it!&nbsp;
But, let’s try something that requires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:12.560
even more finesse. Yes. Putting&nbsp;
cherries in a box. That is really cool!

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.440
Sweeping also works… kind of. As long&nbsp;
as we are looking to sweep beans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:28.160
And, believe it or not, it can even read.&nbsp;
Again, kind of. You see, we can label two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.960
boxes that will contain the good and bad&nbsp;
stuff. And then, instruct the AI as follows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:46.480
This worked well, and it will work, even if we&nbsp;
try to be a bit more tricky, and swap the labels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:05:00.080
And, as you see, it is not falling for it. I&nbsp;
will have to give partial credit for that drop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:07.760
but still, all this from less than 200&nbsp;
demonstrations? That is incredible. So cool!

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:15.200
And, what you see here is what researchers refer&nbsp;
to as sim2real. Teaching an AI something in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:23.280
simulated world, and then, deploying it into the&nbsp;
real world. Okay, so what is all this good for?

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:29.600
Here is an amazing example, look, OpenAI&nbsp;
trained their robot hand in a simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:36.640
to be able to rotate these Rubik cubes. And then,&nbsp;
deployed this software onto a real robot hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:43.120
and, look! It can use this simulation knowledge,&nbsp;
and now it works in the real world too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:52.080
But sim2real has relevance to self-driving cars&nbsp;
too. Look. Tesla is already working on creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.960
virtual worlds, and training their cars there. One&nbsp;
of the advantages of that is that we can create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:06.640
really unlikely and potentially unsafe scenarios,&nbsp;
but, in these virtual worlds, the self-driving AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:13.280
can train itself safely. And when they deploy&nbsp;
them into the real world, they will have all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:20.000
this knowledge. It is fantastic to see that&nbsp;
Waymo is also moving in this direction.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:26.000
As you see, sim2real is real. And&nbsp;
NVIDIA’s new technique also puts us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.600
one step closer to a future&nbsp;
where this kind of intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.720
can be democratized and used by&nbsp;
everyone. What a time to be alive!

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.120
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

